{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact stream-close head 3693c9f81d074c2b0f792959bf2a419a3594602a on builder/stream-http-close-20261103, tree 93970e42, for request 10200327 under promise 414faa98 and review request 928d713d. This approval covers cleanup for a disconnect that the runtime reports. It does not fix the Chromium cleanup failure, does not close parent 01e2c4c4, and claims no deployment, no historical hosted cause and no J5 acceptance change.\n\nScope. Main ecf59e05 is an ancestor, and the worktree is clean. Against main, the head changes the scope route, the new helper http-stream.ts, a sessions.ts comment, the Room dispatcher, the sandbox, eight Worker configurations, four tests, the security inventory and its test, the note and four manifests. The commit-message check passes for both commits. No card path changes. Page run 41669368956 succeeded at the frozen checkpoint b6d8a38e. From b6d8a38e to the head, only the note and the four manifests change.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- closeOnDisconnect adds one abort listener and, on abort, hands the existing cached close to waitUntil once. When the signal is already aborted, it closes at once. It takes no ID, target or new authority.\n- The scope route now calls streamExchange, which runs the same openStream as stream: the signed-read refusal, the object lookup and the object's own session check. The response body and headers stay the same. The route attaches the hook only when a caller supplies a request context, so synthetic and hosted callers behave as before.\n- Body cancellation, EOF and disconnect share one cached close in both the route and the sandbox. The tests show one release RPC when a disconnect overlaps a body cancellation.\n- The sandbox attaches the hook only after the opening returns its headers. An abort during opening therefore closes through the already-aborted check. The 503 timeout path keeps its own cleanup.\n- The eight configurations add enable_request_signal and nothing else. Their compatibility dates stay fixed.\n- The inventory classes streamExchange as a session operation, like stream. The seal changes cover exactly the edited owners and the new helper.\n- The three controls distinguish as stated. Without the route hook, the Native sessions case fails. Without the already-aborted check, only the during-opening route case fails. Without the sandbox hook, both sandbox cases fail. Each control restores the file.\n- The frozen production witness reads a complete 98-byte head and resets its own socket. The runtime reports one disconnect and the route makes one release RPC. Native then shows no open stream, and the head stays unchanged.\n\nCan the patch stand alone? Yes. It adds a close only where the runtime reports a disconnect, and the close it calls already existed. When the runtime reports nothing, the stream behaves as it does on main, so the browser case gets no worse.\n\nLimits that stay open:\n\n1. The Chromium release witness fails with exit 1. After Abort, Native still shows one open stream, and no disconnect signal arrives. The second observer exits 0 only because it finished its observations. Both of its cleanup flags read false, and Native still shows two open streams. This head does not fix that browser transport boundary. Request ac0c3a70 asks the planner to name its owner.\n2. Original parent 01e2c4c4 stays open.\n3. The historical hosted HTTP200-to-TypeError ending has no exact cause. The recorder discarded the cause, code and message. Matching timing supports a client-timeout inference, but nothing proves it. Request eff2fee0 owns that limit.\n4. The first early-abort probe fails with exit 1 because it wrongly required a release RPC, and its record stays as filed. The initial focused run, the type error and the inventory refusals also stay on the record. The wrong caption of 18 Python children stays sealed beside its correction to 17.\n\nThe gate passed. gate.exit at b6d8a38e reads exit 0. source-before and source-after both record tree 29ce1ab8, the same lock and a clean status. All five stages passed with zero unhandled errors.\n\nEvidence. The four manifests list 154 public files. All match their sizes and digests and are read-only, every SHA256SUMS file matches, and no unit holds an unlisted file. All 29 private gate files match their index and their own SHA256SUMS. The scan for private keys, GitHub tokens and bearer values finds no match. The note names the manifests by path, not by digest. The head commits them, so the artifacts pin their bytes.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The route still owns the reader's side of the stream, and the runtime's disconnect signal now reaches the same close capability as body cancellation.\n\nSecurity: no finding. The hook can only release a stream that the route already authorized and opened. The flag has one more effect, which the note does not name: the existing MCP check at packages/mcp/src/http.ts:395 now sees a client that disconnects while a stream opens, and returns 204. The check exists for that case, so the effect is safe.\n\nSimplification: no required change. One observation for the transport follow-up: the hook holds request.signal without the Request. On Node, that loses aborts after a garbage collection. The Workers runtime owns this signal, and the witness passes, but it ran without forcing a collection.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:9db3f51997d7a32e1508be78eb57ece85c3ae643","binding":"assigned","head":"3693c9f81d074c2b0f792959bf2a419a3594602a","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:01e80ebdfec2e31c5b524e32c38450e35de9d4af\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:414faa9888c7da844782808f16c551564c9be4f1\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:01e80ebdfec2e31c5b524e32c38450e35de9d4af","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}